{"timestamp": "2026-09-21T17:26:02.804618", "data": {"url": "https://www.wikihow.com/Search-the-Internet", "title": "How to Search the Internet Like a Pro", "content": "Categories Computers and Electronics Internet Search Engines How to Search the Internet & Find Exactly What You Want Download Article Tips to find something specific on Google & other search engines Co-authored by Tyrone Showers and Hannah Dillon Last Updated: June 20, 2026 Fact Checked Download Article Starting Your Web Search | Refining Your Search Results | Finding Good Search Results | Video | Expert Interview | Q&A | Tips | Show more | Show less X This article was co-authored by Tyrone Showers and by wikiHow staff writer, Hannah Dillon . Tyrone Showers is a Technologist and the Co-owner of Taliferro Group, an IT consulting company based in Seattle, Washington. With over 35 years of professional experience, he specializes in API Design, e-Commerce, Operational Efficiency, and website development. He has a B.S. in Computer Science from DeVry Institute of Technology. There are 13 references cited in this article, which can be found at the bottom of the page. This article has been fact-checked, ensuring the accuracy of any cited facts and confirming the authority of its sources. This article has been viewed 648,123 times. A video of a purring tiger, a 2Pac song remixed with the Thomas the Tank Engine theme, a list of unusual college majors, the number of letters in the longest Spanish word\u2014you can find just about anything on the internet if you know how to look for it! This wikiHow teaches you how to search the web using the most popular search engines and how to improve your queries to get the most relevant results. We also go over how to find the best sources when searching online, with tips from Technologist Tyrone Showers. Quick Steps to Using Search Engines Visit a search engine, like Google or Bing. Type in what you\u2019re looking for in the search bar. Press Enter to run your search and click a result to view it. Add quotation marks if you\u2019re searching for a multi-word phrase. Rephrase your search terms to get better results. Filter your results by date or type to eliminate unhelpful content. Use special search engine operators to narrow down the results. Steps Section 1 of 3: Starting Your Web Search Download Article 1 Go to a search engine. A search engine is a website that collects and organizes information on the internet and makes it available for searching. Search engines use algorithms to display the most relevant search results based on trends, your location, and sometimes even your web activity. Many search engines have their own mobile apps that make searching easier on your phone or tablet. Check out some of the most popular search engines: Google is the most popular search engine in the world. [1] X Research source It's so popular that the word \"Googling\" is often used in place of \"searching the web.\" Google also has special image and video search features that make it easy to find all sorts of media. Bing is Microsoft's answer to Google, and the second most popular search engine. Many of Google's functions, like image and video searching, are also available on Bing. Yahoo used to be the largest search engine in the world. These days, it's powered by Bing and delivers similar results. DuckDuckGo is a privacy-focused search engine that doesn't collect or store your personal information. In fact, this search engine is so privacy-focused that it doesn't even look at your location or IP address. Startpage.com is another search engine focused on privacy, but it uses Google's search engine in the background instead of its own program. [2] X Research source The benefit is that you'll get Google's excellent quality results without sacrificing your personal data. Swisscows is another privacy-focused search engine, but it uses its own software that gives relevant results influenced by semantics. [3] X Research source Yandex is one of the most popular search engines in Russia, but the English version has gained traction in other countries, including the United States. 2 Type what you're looking for into the search bar. You'll find the search bar at the top of most search engines. You can enter a single word, a sentence, a phrase, some numbers, or anything else you wish. [4] X Research source If you're searching for a multi-word phrase, surround it in quotation marks so the search engine knows to keep those words together. For example, \"natural perfumers\" , \"Trader Joe's dried mangos\" . [5] X Research source Sometimes it's helpful to phrase your search like a question, such as What is Cardi B's real name? , What was James Baldwin's last book? , or Is there a vegan Chinese restaurant in Portland? Don't worry about capitalization. If you search \"the lion king\" or \"the new york times,\" your search engine will know you're talking about the Disney movie The Lion King or the newspaper The New York Times . Don't worry about spelling too much. Most search engines are built to understand some minor spelling errors. For example, if you search \"macroni and chees resipee,\" your search engine will guess that you're really looking for a \"macaroni and cheese recipe.\" In fact, you'll likely see a correction at the top of the search engine results page. Even if you severely misspell something, your search engine will try its best to guess what you're trying to look for. If you were actually trying to look for what you originally searched, there is a link at the top of the page you can click that will search for your original query. Advertisement 3 Press the \u21b5 Enter or \u23ce Return key to run your search. This displays your search results in a list. If you're using a smartphone or tablet, you may have to tap Search or Go instead. If you have a KaiOS-based phone, press the center button to run your search. 4 Click or tap a result to view it. If you've found a website that looks like what you want, click or tap it to open it in your browser. To go back to the search results, click or tap your browser's back button. The results display differently depending on what you're looking for. For example, if you searched for the word \"weather\" in Google or Bing, you'll see a weather chart with the local conditions at the top of your results. If you scroll down a little, you'll probably see some news articles about the weather, as well as links to popular weather websites. If you search for an address or location, you'll usually see a map and/or information about the business or landmark. If you scroll down through the first page and don't find what you're looking for, click or tap the next page number or Next at the bottom to view the next set of results. The best results are often on the first page, but sometimes you'll need to dig a little deeper to find that magic result. Advertisement Section 2 of 3: Refining Your Search Results Download Article 1 Rephrase your query for better results. If you're not finding the right information, you might need to use different words in your search. It's also possible that what you're searching for is too specific or too broad. [6] X Research source For example, if you searched for most popular search engines and were hoping to see Canada-specific results, try most popular search engine in Canada or most popular search engine in Canada 2026 instead. You can also rephrase your search query to affect what type of results you get. For example, if you want to look up medical articles on how to deal with headaches that you get all the time, try searching \"chronic headaches\" versus \"my head hurts a lot,\" since chronic headaches is a term that a medical website would use. 2 Filter your results by date. The majority of search engines have some handy (but easy to miss) tools near the top of the results pages, including the ability to only show results from a certain time period. Click the Any time menu at the top of the search results page (or click the filter icon in Yandex) to choose a date range, such as Past 24 hours or Past year . [7] X Research source If you're using Google and don't see the Any time menu, click the Tools tab at the top first. You can also use dates in your search queries, such as Billboard July 2010 charts . 3 Filter your results by type. In general, doing a regular internet search displays a list of web links that match what you've typed. But what if you just want to see photos, videos, or news articles instead? Fortunately, you can click Images , Videos , News , and sometimes other categories at the top of every search engine's results page to filter your results. [8] X Research source Google and Bing both have a bunch of other options, including Maps and Shopping . Most search engines have great photo search pages that let you specify things like photo size and colors. Use the menus at the top of the search results page to get the best results. 4 Use special search engine operators. Operators are special words and symbols you can include in your searches so they'll return more relevant results. Many operators work for all search engines, but some occasionally vary from the standard. [9] X Research source If you're searching for a set of words, such as a song lyric or a sentence that appeared verbatim in a news article, put quotes (\") around the query. \"I woke up like this\" , \"Definitions belong to the definers, not the defined\" . If there's a certain word messing up your results (for example, if you're trying to find information on cardinals but are only seeing results for the St. Louis Cardinals), Showers says you can type a minus sign (-) in front of the word or phrase you want to omit. [10] X Expert Source Tyrone Showers Technologist Expert Interview In this example, you might type cardinals -\"St. Louis\" . You can use the word \"AND\" (or the & symbol) to ensure that two different words or phrases both appear in the same result. For example, coronavirus AND \"swine flu\" . You can also use \"NOT\" to make sure results don't include a certain word (e.g., coronavirus NOT \"swine flu\" ). [11] X Trustworthy Source Microsoft Support Technical support and product information from Microsoft. Go to source They can even be used together, as well as multiple times per query. Make sure \"AND\" and \"NOT\" are capitalized, as most search engines will ignore them otherwise. To learn more about using special search engine operators in Google, check out our article on Google \" dorks \" (which have a funny name, but they can help you search Google more effectively). 5 Use the Advanced Search form. So what happens if you still can't find what you're looking for? Well, for one thing, you can always try a different search engine. But if you're pretty devoted to Google, Yahoo, or DuckDuckGo and want to bring out the big guns, go to the site's Advanced Search form. These forms let you enter your search criteria in an extremely specific way, which is great for research that requires only certain types of sources. Select your favorite search engine from the links below to get started: Google Advanced Search Startpage Advanced Search Advertisement Section 3 of 3: Finding Good Search Results Download Article 1 Know where the search page results start. When you search for something on a search engine, you'll see a lot of information on the page, and it can be overwhelming to know where to start. Each search engine is slightly different, but most have the same features. This is what you might find on Google; you'll see similar things on other search engines: Ads: If you see a section at the top of the page that says \"Sponsored,\" these are ads. That means the owner of that website has paid Google to place their website higher up on the search results page. You can click on these links, but know that they may not be the most relevant to what you're searching for. AI Overview: The AI Overview is a section at the top of the page where Google's AI attempts to summarize what you're searching for. Not every search query will trigger an AI Overview, but most do. While this information is sometimes correct, it can also sometimes be very wrong (AI has a tendency to make things up [12] X Research source ), and even dangerous. [13] X Research source While you can look at the AI Overview, it's best to click on the sources linked in the AI Overview instead of taking what the AI Overview says at face value. Featured snippets: If you don't see an AI Overview, you may instead see a featured snippet at the top of the page. The featured snippet takes a segment from a relevant webpage and places it at the top of the page. If this information seems relevant, you can click it, otherwise keep scrolling. Knowledge panels: When you look up an animal, person, event, or any variety of things, you may see a knowledge panel on the right-hand side of the page. This panel will give you information about what you're searching for, and the site it was sourced from will be linked. You can click on this if you want to learn more, or you can continue to the search results. [14] X Research source 2 Understand how search engines work. Search engines work by crawling the internet and indexing webpages. This index is used by an algorithm that takes your search query and ranks it, and serves you the most relevant result. [15] X Research source That means that the results closer to the first page are the best results, according to the search engine's algorithm. 3 Know which results to click on\u2014and which not to. While most websites you'll encounter are completely safe, there are some you should avoid. If anything in the little snippet on the search engine results page seems off, don't click it. For example, if the webpage's title is weird or the webpage URL (the web address printed under the webpage title) is a bunch of random letters, don't click on that search result. And if the steps above have been helpful, be sure to download this article as a PDF so you can print, read, or reference it any time. Download PDF for $2.99 Advertisement Community Q&A Search Add New Question Question Why do need to enter keywords in my search? Community Answer Keywords allow you to get more specific results. If you simply typed in your question, you might get some results that are completely unrelated to what you are looking for. Thanks! We're glad this was helpful. Thank you for your feedback. If wikiHow has helped you, please consider a small contribution to support us in helping more readers like you. We\u2019re committed to providing the world with free how-to resources, and even $1 helps us in our mission. Support wikiHow Yes No Not Helpful 21 Helpful 47 Question What is an e-mail, and how do I use it? Community Answer An email is an address where you can receive mail to electronically instead of getting letters the old-fashioned way through the post office. Simply find an email provider you like (Google is a popular one), sign up, create an email you like, and then you can send emails to others and receive them yourself. Thanks! We're glad this was helpful. Thank you for your feedback. If wikiHow has helped you, please consider a small contribution to support us in helping more readers like you. We\u2019re committed to providing the world with free how-to resources, and even $1 helps us in our mission. Support wikiHow Yes No Not Helpful 21 Helpful 25 Question What are the search engines I can use on the internet? Community Answer Google.com and Bing.com are two of the most popular search engines. If you want others, do a search for other search engines. Thanks! We're glad this was helpful. Thank you for your feedback. If wikiHow has helped you, please consider a small contribution to support us in helping more readers like you. We\u2019re committed to providing the world with free how-to resources, and even $1 helps us in our mission. Support wikiHow Yes No Not Helpful 30 Helpful 30 See more answers Ask a Question 200 characters left Include your email address to get a message when this question is answered. Submit Advertisement Video Tips In addition to Google, most other search engines all have their own downloadable search apps, including Yahoo, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Yandex, and Startpage. Thanks Helpful 0 Not Helpful 1 Android and KaiOS-enabled phones and tablets come with the Google app already installed. To open the Google app, select the multicolored \"G\" icon in your app list. Thanks Helpful 0 Not Helpful 0 Tips from our Readers The advice in this section is based on the lived experiences of wikiHow readers like you. If you have a helpful tip you\u2019d like to share on wikiHow, please submit it in the field below. On Google, you can search for specific URLs that contain your keyword by adding \"inurl:\" before your search word or phrase. Add \".pdf\" at the end of your search to get results that are in pdf format. Submit a Tip All tip submissions are carefully reviewed before being published Name Please provide your name and last initial Submit Thanks for submitting a tip for review! Advertisement You Might Also Like How to Search Google Like a Pro: Tips & Tricks to Find What You Want Find Exactly What You Need on Google with These Advanced Search Tricks How to Find Information Online 5 Ways to Use the Yahoo Search Engine Google Basics: Search, Mail, Maps & More How to Do Internet Research A Comprehensive Guide to Using Bing (Searching, Using + More) How to Find the Answer to Most Questions Change Your Default Search Engine in Any Web Browser or Device Bing Image Search Guide: Find Photos, Search by Image, & More How to Use Google Dorks In Your Searches: Complete Guide How to Search Tweets from a Specific User How to Search a Website for a Word: 3 Easy Methods How to Use Google Scholar for Academic Research Advertisement Expert Interview Thanks for reading our article! If you\u2019d like to learn more about search engines, check out our in-depth interview with Tyrone 